course: dictionary
lesson: 3494
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word fare is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. The clues are the context, and the context here is what the sentence is about.
explain: «Food £45, bus fares £20, power £15.» Money is being counted, so bus fares must be the money you pay to ride the bus. Next, «How did you fare in the test?» asks how you got on, so fare can also mean to do or get on in a certain way.
explain: The third meaning is food. «The Rat makes large allowances because Toad had been on prison fare.» Prison fare is the food served in prison. Check each meaning by putting it into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: A fare is the money you pay to travel on a bus, train, plane, taxi or boat.
fact: To fare means to do or get on in a certain way, as in «fare well».
fact: Fare can also mean food, especially a kind of food, such as simple country fare.
fact: Useful phrases include bus fare, train fare, child fare, return fare, single fare and half fare.
fact: Context tells you which meaning of fare the writer chose.
exercise: «Food £45, bus fares £20, power £15.» What are bus fares in this list? | Food eaten on the bus | A bus driver's job | * Money paid to travel by bus | Times when the bus is late
exercise: Toad had been «on prison fare» for a long time. What is prison fare? | * The food served in prison | A prison uniform | A way of getting on well in prison | Money paid to visit a prison
exercise: Find and copy the word that means money paid for a ride. «A tap of a card pays your fare by radio.» | card | radio | * fare | pays
test: «How did you fare in the test?» What does fare mean here? | Food for lunch | * To do or get on in a certain way | A kind of bus | Money paid for a ride
test: Which phrase means to do well? | Half fare | * Fare well | Simple country fare | Child fare
exam: In the budget list «rent, power, food, bus fares», which meaning of fare fits? | To get on well with others | Food for the family dinner | * Money paid to ride the bus | A bus stop sign with a GPS
